{
 "war": "first-italo-ethiopian-war",
 "start": "1895-01-12",
 "end": "1896-10-26",
 "note": "Italian-held ground in Tigray and the Ethiopian army in Eritrea are drawn as occupied areas over the 1895–96 borders; their edges are approximate reconstructions from the campaign narratives, and the Italians held towns and roads rather than the whole countryside.",
 "chapters": [
  {
   "id": "coatit",
   "title": "Coatit and Senafe",
   "start": "1895-01-12",
   "end": "1895-01-20",
   "bbox": [
    38.6,
    14.1,
    40.1,
    15.7
   ],
   "arrows": [
    "Ras Mengesha invades Eritrea (January 1895)",
    "Baratieri pursues Mengesha to Senafe (January 1895)"
   ],
   "text": "Italy had ruled the colony of Eritrea since the late 1880s and claimed that the Treaty of Wuchale made all of Ethiopia its protectorate, a claim Emperor Menelik II rejected. In January 1895 Ras Mengesha, ruler of the border province of Tigray, crossed into Eritrea. Governor Oreste Baratieri's small colonial army, mostly Eritrean askaris, beat him at Coatit on 13 January and broke up his retreating army at Senafe two days later."
  },
  {
   "id": "tigray",
   "title": "Italy occupies Tigray",
   "start": "1895-03-20",
   "end": "1895-11-30",
   "bbox": [
    38.4,
    12.7,
    40.2,
    14.9
   ],
   "arrows": [
    "Italian advance to Adigrat and Adwa (March–April 1895)",
    "Italian advance to Mekelle and Amba Alagi (October 1895)"
   ],
   "text": "Baratieri crossed into Tigray, occupying Adigrat on 25 March and Adwa on 2 April 1895, and declared the province annexed to Eritrea. After the rains his troops pushed south, forcing the pass at Debra Ailà on 9 October. Mekelle, the Tigrayan capital, was occupied without a fight and fortified, and outposts went on to the mountain of Amba Alagi. Meanwhile Menelik called the whole empire to arms."
  },
  {
   "id": "amba-alagi",
   "title": "Amba Alagi",
   "start": "1895-12-01",
   "end": "1895-12-14",
   "bbox": [
    38.9,
    12.6,
    40.1,
    14.4
   ],
   "arrows": [
    "Ras Makonnen's advance on Amba Alagi (December 1895)"
   ],
   "text": "Menelik's vanguard under Ras Makonnen, joined by Ras Mengesha, reached Amba Alagi in early December. On 7 December 1895 it overran Major Toselli's 2,000 askaris, killing Toselli and most of his men. General Arimondi gathered the survivors, left a garrison in the half-built fort at Mekelle and fell back to Adigrat, where Baratieri was concentrating his army."
  },
  {
   "id": "mekelle",
   "title": "The siege of Mekelle",
   "start": "1895-12-15",
   "end": "1896-01-25",
   "bbox": [
    38.9,
    13.1,
    40.0,
    14.6
   ],
   "arrows": [
    "Ethiopian advance to Mekelle (December 1895)"
   ],
   "text": "Ras Makonnen surrounded the fort above Mekelle on 18 December, and Menelik arrived with the main army in early January 1896. After two weeks of bombardment the Ethiopians cut the garrison off from its water, and on 21 January Major Galliano agreed to surrender. Menelik let the garrison march out with its weapons to rejoin Baratieri."
  },
  {
   "id": "manoeuvre",
   "title": "The road to Adwa",
   "start": "1896-01-26",
   "end": "1896-02-29",
   "bbox": [
    38.5,
    13.4,
    39.95,
    14.8
   ],
   "arrows": [
    "Menelik's army moves from Mekelle to Adwa (January–February 1896)"
   ],
   "text": "Instead of attacking the Italian base at Adigrat, Menelik led his army west by way of Hawzen to the plain around Adwa. Baratieri, fearing an invasion of Eritrea, moved his army west from Adigrat to face him. Both armies were running out of food, and on 29 February Baratieri and his generals decided to attack rather than retreat."
  },
  {
   "id": "adwa",
   "title": "The Battle of Adwa",
   "start": "1896-02-29",
   "end": "1896-03-05",
   "bbox": [
    38.6,
    13.95,
    39.7,
    14.95
   ],
   "arrows": [
    "Italian night advance on Adwa (29 February – 1 March 1896)",
    "Italian retreat to Eritrea (March 1896)"
   ],
   "text": "About 17,700 Italian and Eritrean troops marched by night on separate mountain tracks and lost touch with each other. At dawn on 1 March 1896 Menelik's army, perhaps 73,000 to 100,000 strong and well armed with modern rifles, attacked each brigade in turn. By noon the Italians were fleeing towards Eritrea, leaving over 6,000 dead, about 3,800 prisoners and all their artillery; only the garrison at Adigrat held on."
  },
  {
   "id": "peace",
   "title": "Retreat and peace",
   "start": "1896-03-06",
   "end": "1896-10-26",
   "bbox": [
    38.4,
    13.9,
    39.9,
    15.5
   ],
   "arrows": [],
   "text": "The defeat brought down Prime Minister Crispi's government in Rome. General Baldissera reorganised the army in Eritrea and relieved the Adigrat garrison, but Menelik insisted on its surrender as a condition of peace, and the Italians left Adigrat on 18 May 1896. In the Treaty of Addis Ababa of 26 October Italy recognised Ethiopia's full independence and kept Eritrea north of the Mareb river."
  }
 ],
